{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.FHWExponentWindow","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.FHWExponentWindow","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.MobiusMutualChannel","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Pow.Real","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Log.Basic"],"declarations":7,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FHWExponentWindow.neg_two_satisfies_both","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FHWExponentWindow.neg_two_satisfies_both","kind":"theorem","line":183,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FHWExponentWindow.lean#L183-L199","doc":"The converse half: `−2` really does satisfy both criteria, so the\nintersection is the nonempty set `{−2}` rather than empty. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FHWExponentWindow.bfhw_window_mobius_unique","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FHWExponentWindow.bfhw_window_mobius_unique","kind":"theorem","line":166,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FHWExponentWindow.lean#L166-L181","doc":"**THE RESULT: the two criteria intersect in exactly one exponent.**\n\nAn exponent that is admissible to BFHW *and* Möbius invariant is `−2`, and\n`−2` is admissible — so the conformal criterion selects precisely the\nendpoint of the topological window.\n\nTwo things make this more than a restatement. The window is a genuine\ninterval (`bfhwWindow_not_singleton`), so the endpoint is not the only\ncandidate on offer. And the two criteria are independent in origin: BFHW's\ncomes from finiteness and an energy barrier to changing knot type, §7's from\ninvariance under inversions. Neither was derived from the other, and the\ncorpus holds the paper that states the first. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FHWExponentWindow.mobiusInvariant_rpow_exponent_eq_neg_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FHWExponentWindow.mobiusInvariant_rpow_exponent_eq_neg_two","kind":"theorem","line":138,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FHWExponentWindow.lean#L138-L164","doc":"**Möbius invariance forces the exponent to be exactly `−2`.**\n\n§7 of `MobiusMutualChannel` gives `f d = f 1 / d ^ 2` for any Möbius\ninvariant kernel. Specialising to the power law `f d = d ^ e` and evaluating\nat `d = 2` gives `2 ^ e = 2 ^ (−2)`; taking logarithms and cancelling\n`log 2 ≠ 0` yields the exponent.\n\nNote what is *not* assumed: no window, no finiteness, no barrier. The\nconformal symmetry alone determines the exponent. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FHWExponentWindow.neg_one_not_mem_bfhwWindow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FHWExponentWindow.neg_one_not_mem_bfhwWindow","kind":"theorem","line":133,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FHWExponentWindow.lean#L133-L136","doc":"**The Newtonian exponent is excluded by BFHW**, as their remark says — a\nsanity check that the window encodes what the paper claims. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FHWExponentWindow.bfhwWindow_not_singleton","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FHWExponentWindow.bfhwWindow_not_singleton","kind":"theorem","line":123,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FHWExponentWindow.lean#L123-L131","doc":"The window is a genuine interval, not a point: `−5/2` is admissible to BFHW\nand is **not** `−2`.\n\nThis is the load-bearing non-degeneracy check. Without it, \"Möbius\ninvariance picks out the endpoint\" would be empty — the endpoint might be\nthe only thing there. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FHWExponentWindow.neg_two_mem_bfhwWindow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FHWExponentWindow.neg_two_mem_bfhwWindow","kind":"theorem","line":118,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FHWExponentWindow.lean#L118-L121","doc":"`−2` lies in the BFHW window — the window is inhabited at the endpoint, so\nthe uniqueness statement below is not vacuously true. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FHWExponentWindow.BFHWWindow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FHWExponentWindow.BFHWWindow","kind":"def","line":111,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FHWExponentWindow.lean#L111-L116","doc":"**The BFHW admissible window**, as *cited* from Bull. AMS 28 (1993) 99–103.\n\n`e > −3` is their finiteness condition on smooth simple loops; `e ≤ −2` is\ntheir infinite-energy-barrier condition. This predicate **records** the two\nclaims so the intersection below can be stated; it does not derive them. ","signatureChunk":0}]}